cases.input:
analyze comments on this post. The post was my essay arguing that most prompt injection defenses fail because they try to filter inputs instead of limiting what the agent can do. Comments from the discussion thread on example.com:

@arlo_k: This is the cleanest framing I've read. Capability limits over input filters. Sharing with my team.
@nina_sec: I agree with the conclusion but the essay hand-waves how you limit capabilities when the whole point of the agent is to browse and email.
@tcole: Input filtering still has value as defense in depth. Saying it 'fails' is overstated.
@m_okafor: You never mention the confused deputy problem, which is literally this. Feels like reinvention without credit.
@quill: Great read, but the middle section on tool scoping drags.
@reed77: Disagree. Filters caught 4 of the 6 injections in our red team last quarter. Not nothing.
@dana_w: The example of the calendar agent leaking invites was vivid and convincing.
@bytewise: Strong title, weak evidence. Where is the data?
@h_lindqvist: Sent this to our CISO. Practical, and the right level of alarm.
